WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.980
[Heda Kovaly] My father's name was Irving Bloch,

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:12.180
my mother was Marta,

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.500
and I had a brother, Georg.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.620
Both my grandparents were farmers,

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:20.420
which was quite unusual for a Jewish family.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.960
Both of my grandparents had farms

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.860
and my parents grew up there.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.860
I remember my grandmother very well.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:32.920
She was gorgeous—white hair, very lovely.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:39.860
And she was, um, killed when she was 
86 years old by the Germans.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.700
There was, um, very little food,

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:54.120
the work was dreadfully hard,

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.740
and it was completely closed.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.780
It was so claustrophobic and there was 
absolutely not enough food.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:13.780
For me, Lodz was especially horrible

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:15.980
because of the children.

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:17.560
They cried, and cried,

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:21.520
and then they stopped crying one day.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.560
They executed—

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:25.760
I don't know how many people,

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:29.640
because I never had the courage to go and look.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.840
Becau—they let them hang

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.900
on the square for several days.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.360
And my mother went there,

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.140
because she said someone

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.580
has to pray for them.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.060
From time to time, the Germans came

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:48.260
and they took people away.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:06.480
The Germans went from one place to the other.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:09.900
And then they let us go in the street.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.557
And there was this—

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:14.380
always this officer—

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.840
a German officer—

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:18.820
who

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:20.740
said this person go this way,

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.940
and this person goes back.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:25.460
And they took all the

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:26.714
old people,

00:02:26.714 --> 00:02:29.247
and the children, and took them away.

00:02:37.604 --> 00:02:40.476
We went out of the train.

00:02:40.476 --> 00:02:41.274
And they—

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.140
right there, they started dividing women from men.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:46.540
And I only could see my father's face.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.671
And he said, “Take care of Mother.”

00:02:49.671 --> 00:02:50.688
And that was all.

00:02:50.688 --> 00:02:54.198
And I was—you know, I didn't see him walking away.

00:02:54.198 --> 00:02:56.731
It was just such a crowd, you see?

00:02:56.731 --> 00:02:58.646
And, um,

00:02:58.646 --> 00:03:02.740
we walked, hand-in-hand, with my mother.

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:05.060
And there was Mengele.

00:03:05.068 --> 00:03:09.994
And he showed my mother the right side.

00:03:09.994 --> 00:03:11.749
And I wanted to go with her.

00:03:11.749 --> 00:03:13.080
And there was a soldier, and

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:16.556
he threw me to the ground, and said, "You stay here.”

00:03:16.556 --> 00:03:18.789
“Where are these people going?

00:03:18.789 --> 00:03:20.485
What's going to happen to them?”

00:03:20.485 --> 00:03:21.901
And he said, “Don't be hysterical.

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:24.434
And if you ask, you aren’t going to see them again.”

00:03:26.009 --> 00:03:28.921
Very vividly, I can—I can see

00:03:29.819 --> 00:03:31.454
pictures, like in a movie.

00:03:31.454 --> 00:03:33.987
You know, I can see details,

00:03:33.987 --> 00:03:36.500
but I have a—there is no connection.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.155
And the last, um,

00:03:40.429 --> 00:03:42.842
uh, night, um...

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.335
I don't think

00:03:45.375 --> 00:03:48.068
I spoke to anybody the whole time 
I was in Auschwitz.

00:03:48.068 --> 00:03:50.541
I just lost all, uh,

00:03:50.541 --> 00:03:53.971
capability of communicating with people.

00:03:53.971 --> 00:03:57.202
And I sort of crawled aside.

00:03:57.202 --> 00:03:59.676
And I thought, my mother.

00:03:59.676 --> 00:04:04.721
I couldn't think about anything except
my mother.

00:04:04.721 --> 00:04:07.494
Moje máma.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.020
And I remember sitting on the ground

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:12.500
and hold up my hand and

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:15.711
there was this big, um—you know the dandelions?

00:04:15.711 --> 00:04:21.794
And it came to me and it landed on my hand. 
And I thought, my mother, she's here.

00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:24.706
I thought maybe she just died that moment.

